window.docContents[91].resolve({"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.PqDirNoMatch/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___PqDirNoMatch":{"contents":"Paper › LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession › PqDirNoMatch.leanTier 1 · statement file\n\nTier 1 · statement file used by no headline theorem 100 lines trust banner B1, trusted: the statement source ↗Listed with the statement files, but no headline theorem uses it. None of its declarations is in the closure of a headline theorem of either proof. Tier 1 lists it, as the closure report does: the report lists every statement file (defaultStatementFiles in scripts/TrustedClosure.lean). Proofs are cut after := and shown as ⋯; Lean checks them.\n\n\n\nBob's pre-key recv over a session that does not match: CliSpecPqxdhNoMatchTrusted: the statement. Extends CliSpecPqxdh (PqDir.lean) to recv <peer> of a canonical pre-key message m when the party holds a non-matching session (¬ PqSessionMatches, DEV-CLI11; see Computational/docs/TRUSTED_BASE.md).When promote_matching_session finds no matching session (and encodeRecordUnderExtras encodes no previous sessions), libsignal runs a fresh PQXDH handshake independent of the existing session, archives the old session on success, and decrypts the inner message solely under the new session. Accordingly, CliSpecPqxdhNoMatch relates the outcome to pqRecvPrekeyModel at { st with session := none }:* on accept: exit 0 with the no-session model's stdout and files at every canonical path except sessionFile (partyAddress st.party.other) (Outcome.AgreeExcept);* on reject or panic: exact agreement with pqxdhExpectedOutcome (exit 1 with all files unchanged, or aborted).\n\n\n\ndef Outcome.AgreeExceptL40–51 ↗#Two outcomes agree at every path except p: both abort, or both exit with the same code and stdout, and their files agree at every path that is not a spelling of p, i.e. whose canonical form (Arm64.normalizePath) differs from p's. Paths are compared in canonical form because the files of both outcomes are read through fsView (Run.lean), which looks a path up by its canonical form: with raw String inequality, another spelling of p (e.g. \"./\" ++ p) would be a path \"other than p\" at which fsView reads p's file, so the files would have to agree at p too.\n\ndef OutcomeMatchesModelExceptPathL53–61 ↗#The run's outcome o against the model's answer r on the inputs i, the file at p excepted on an accept: if r accepts, o agrees with pqxdhExpectedOutcome W i r at every path except p (Outcome.AgreeExcept); if r rejects or panics, o is pqxdhExpectedOutcome W i r (exit 1 with every file unchanged, or abort).\n\ndef CliSpecPqxdhNoMatchL63–101 ↗#Bob's pre-key recv over a session that does not match (run is any implementation; the real one is the engine process of the CLI's ELF). For every model primitives W and model extras X, every party state st in the domain (PqxdhPartyInSpecDomain) that holds a session (x, g), and every typed pre-key message m that the session does not match (¬ PqSessionMatches x g m) whose inner message is below 2^32 - 2048 bytes, with coins rc: if (3 ‖ m.bytes).length > stdinCapBytes (2^20), recv <peer> exits 2 (read_stdin cap) with empty stdout and every file unchanged (the session file included); otherwise the run of recv <peer> of 3 ‖ m.bytes has an outcome, and it is pqRecvPrekeyModel X W on st with no session, the session file excepted on an accept (OutcomeMatchesModelExceptPath). m's registration id, ids, keys and inner message are any values (the adversary's).DEV-CLI11 (libsignal): after a pre-key recv over a session that does not match it is accepted, the session file and the party's later calls are not claimed. libsignal archives the old session as the new record's previous session, which encodeRecordUnderExtras cannot write (one current session, DEV-C-PREV). So on an accept the session file's bytes are excepted (OutcomeMatchesModelExceptPath), and the party's directory is then outside PqxdhPartyInSpecDomain: no spec covers its later calls. A game must exclude them, and leaks of that file, until the file is deleted. No trusted statement about the binary claims the bytes of this session file: CliPqxdhElfCorrectPlaced (CliBinaryPq.lean) excepts them as this spec does. In the CLI refinement (Symbolic/Specifications/Refinement/Statement.lean), Bob makes no call after such an accept until the environment deletes the file (BobCallAllowed, D2(c)), and the file's current session may be leaked meanwhile. A claim about the file or those calls would be a separate statement over the same inputs, and this one would not change.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"LibsignalBinary/Specifications/SignalSession/PqDirNoMatch.lean","id":"/Tier-1___-statements/Statement-files-_LPAR_T_RPAR_/LibsignalBinary.Specifications.SignalSession.PqDirNoMatch/#file-LibsignalBinary___Specifications___SignalSession___PqDirNoMatch"}});